        "canonical_field": "essay:generated:7f47df13-6fac-4df1-b1b5-801c2b080879",
        "expected": "What draws me to Applied Intuition is that the company sells tools and infrastructure, not demos. Most of my work has ended up in the same place. I built a Playwright-based job-application agent where every mutating capability sits behind fail-closed flags like SUBMIT_ENABLED, and where capabilities move up an explicit validation ladder from unit fixtures to live mutation, because a system should not claim to work just because it passed against a local fixture. That instinct feels close to what autonomy tooling has to do: decide what a system is actually allowed to act on, and prove it against the level of reality it claims to handle. I also work on the machine-learning core of an anomaly-detection and alert-prioritization system, including temporal sequence modeling over event streams and a learning-to-rank layer, because an anomaly system that fires too often creates a second problem instead of solving the first. Applied Intuition serves customers whose failure modes are physical, which makes evaluation and prioritization real engineering constraints rather than nice-to-haves. I want to own substantial pieces of a system in that kind of environment, working on infrastructure that other engineers depend on to know whether something is safe to ship.",
        "observed": "What draws me to Applied Intuition is that the company sells tools and infrastructure, not demos. Most of my work has ended up in the same place. I built a Playwright-based job-application agent where every mutating capability sits behind fail-closed flags like SUBMIT_ENABLED, and where capabilities move up an explicit validation ladder from unit fixtures to live mutation, because a system should not claim to work just because it passed against a local fixture. That instinct feels close to what autonomy tooling has to do: decide what a system is actually allowed to act on, and prove it against the level of reality it claims to handle. I also work on the machine-learning core of an anomaly-detection and alert-prioritization system, including temporal sequence modeling over event streams and a learning-to-rank layer, because an anomaly system that fires too often creates a second problem instead of solving the first. Applied Intuition serves customers whose failure modes are physical, which makes evaluation and prioritization real engineering constraints rather than nice-to-haves. I want to own substantial pieces of a system in that kind of environment, working on infrastructure that other engineers depend on to know whether something is safe to ship.",
